On Thu, 2 Nov 2006, Malcolm Ryan wrote:
> Gary Olson wrote:
>> The unihorn ability is more of a healer function than an Alchemy function.
>
> Yes, but it is in keeping with it's role as the giver of life.
>
>> UNLESS, a different mythos is implemented. From "FullMetal Alchemist",
>> "The obtained Stone is not bound to a fixed form". In Nethack,
>> implementation would mean the Stone randomly changes gem types. This
>> would allow for a greater implementation of the gem alchemy you
>> mentioned. The Alchemist would have to keep looking at his stone to see
>> if the Stone had transmuted itself into a form which was useful.
>
> How about this: when dipped in a potion other than water, the stone
> transmutes into the appropriate gem for creating that potion (according
> to existing gem alchemy rules). Then when it is dipped in water, a
> succesful alchemy check will convert the water into a potion of the
> appropriate type (an unsuccesful check will produce a random potion, or
> cause an explosion).
>
> Should transmuting the stone use up the orignal potion? What if it
> turned the potion into water as the magic was absorbed into the stone?
> Then it could also be useful in creating holy water without a fountain.

   If the stone used up the original potion, then you'd have
(stone + potion + water) = (magic gem + water) = (stone + potion),
which is obviously a bad deal, and nobody would use it. If the stone
turned the original potion into water, you'd have
(stone + potion + water) = (magic gem + 2*water) = (stone + 2*potion),
which, assuming that that's the way potion alchemy works, actually
seems like a good idea to me! (If #dipping the gem converts only one
of the stack, then obviously this is also a stupid idea.)

   Notice that this means that an infinite supply of water becomes
an infinite supply of potion X, given that the player has acquired
at least one potion X by other means.

   Also notice that you can't use the stone to "blank" an infinite
number of potions into water, because you would need the same number
of potions of water to change the stone back from a gem... unless
you could "blank" the stone by other means. Obvious ways to do it
would be:

   * cancel the stone (okay, since you could just as well cancel the
original potion)
   * drop the stone in a pool (okay, since pools can dilute potions
en masse anyway)

   So, a Philosopher's Stone that took potion powers from one potion
and let you put the powers into a stack of waters sounds like a
very cool idea to me. It's powerful, but then it's a Quest Artifact.

   What would be the appearance of the Stone in gem form? Just "a ruby",
or "a ruby named Philosopher's Stone", or "the ruby Philosopher's
Stone", or maybe "the Philosopher's Ruby"?

my $.02,
